  "transcript": "Tutor: Okay. Explain Kahneman's System one and System two thinking and give a convincing example of each.\nChild: [tongue click ]So system one is all about... Uh, it's that fast, intuitive, reactionary response to any sort of... [sigh ]external stimulus\nTutor: Mm-hmm.\nChild: Um, think about driving and someone cuts you\nTutor: Yeah.\nChild: off, your- your immediate reaction might be to be upset and\nChild: wanna do something. Um, system two is all about that thoughtful\nChild: , slower\nTutor: Mm-hmm.\nChild: Uh, more considered approach where you're thinking through the ,what just happened and- and then taking your time to formulate a response... That\nChild: may be saying\nTutor: Go on.\nChild: You know, I don't need to get upset here that someone cut me off. It happened and\nChild: ... And I can get past it\nTutor: Yeah, that's a strong example. System one is that snap reaction. And system two is taking a breath and working\nTutor: through it step by step.\nTutor: How does the thinking play out in the moment for you in that switch\nTutor: from the quick reaction to the more careful check?\nChild: [tongue click ]How does it play out\nChild: ? [tongue click] Um\nChild: ... I guess you're just flooded with thoughts on... and all the different\nChild: [breath ]all the different ways you can react to a certain situation and then System two thinking's all about optimizing and picking the best, the best response available that you've come up with\nTutor: Yeah, that lands for me. You've shown the quick gut reaction and then the slower sift through options to choose a response.",
